  {
    "episode_id": 65052,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/6/5/65052.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 11,
    "turn_text": "Well, one of the biggest complaints that I'm hearing from coaches all over the country, assistant coaches and head coaches and athletic directors is the possibility that some coaches are going out and offering inducements to players in their homes or whatever, making a certain amount of money for the first year or second year or third or fourth year. And they can't really back that up. I know the collectives, the groups across the country that are being hired by some of the universities are really the ones that are supposed to do that. But we got to get that out of our recruiting inducement. Again, once they get there, they should be able, like the Supreme Court says, make money off of their name, image, and likeness, but not for a recruiting inducement to what school that they're going to go to. The other problem we're having is while this thing is originated in the last couple of years of the NIO, now we have the transfer report. And you put those two together, and you're asking for huge problems down the line. So we're not only working with name, image, and likeness, but we're also looking at this transfer portal of out of make everybody, as many people as we can, happy to make it fair for everybody to be able to recruit and possibly keep players on their campus through number one, where they can stay and get an education. Because say you get 40 hours at one school and you transfer, you might lose all those hours. And you have to start over. And there's no way you're going to get a degree after that. It doesn't happen in college sports. So we've got to look at the degree, we've got to look at education, and we've got to look at the well-being with student athletes.",
