    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/6/5/65052.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 13,
    "turn_text": "yeah we might not be able to get consensus once we get all information and after this letter we sent out and again people they want to put their two cents worth and that's fine now we're going to have between his staff and my staff and some of the committee staff, we'll probably have over 100 people working on this from Washington DC. That's how it happens up there. You don't write a bill, a couple people write this up. It is complex to get it done. And then you have to sell it through committee. And then you have to sell it to the individual centers across the country representing their states. And so if we plan on getting anything passed, there's going to have to be a lot of a lot of self-manship to where Joe Manchin, who played college football, he understands sports. Myself being the only one up here that's worked with NCAA, worked with commissioners, worked with athletic directors. We've just got to do the right thing for everybody, but it's not going to be easy to put that together. Joe and I talk pretty much every day. we built a pretty good relationship over the last year. So people are going to trust him on the left. I hope that people trust me on the right that we're doing it for the right thing. But we're not trying to pull the wool over and buy it. And as I said, at the very beginning, I wish we didn't have to do this. But the NCAA looks like that they're afraid of lawsuits and they can't get everybody on board. We're not going to be able to clarify on board, but we're going to do the best we possibly can. If we get a consensus, if we think we can do the right thing, at the end of the day, we might not be able to do it. We might, Joe and I might look at each other and I go, hey, this is impossible. We can't get enough people on board to get this done, but I hope we can, and we're trying. This time last year, I said, no, we're, I had coaches calling man. So no, we're staying out of this. You don't want us involved. But after a year and see what's going on, it is getting worse. It's going to destroy college sports as we know it. Men and women, you're going to end up with smaller groups, smaller scholarships, less money in the universities. So these aren't going to be as good. There's going to have to probably be some people that that's going to have to understand that they're going to have to work hard behind the scenes to sell this in some form or fashion. But again, we're going to give it the college try. We're going to do the best we possibly can to make this work because I think this college sports is one of the reasons our country is as successful as it is.",
