    "turn_idx": 17,
    "turn_text": "No, you're right. It should be simpler than what we're stating. Again, we're not getting into the money part. We want to get into the scenario of when you can do it, why you can do it, how you can do it, basically. As we said, the Supreme Court said they make money. So again, that's going to happen. We just want to make sure that we cover all bases to kind of put some parameters on what we used to have when I was in in coaching, all my coaches here, we had a recruiting calendar. When you could start doing things, when you could evaluate, when you could talk to players, how many times you could talk to them, when the signage were, what they could do as student athletes, and you just can't wipe all the rules off the rule book. We got to have some stipulations for what you can do, what assistance can do. And then, of course, you're talking about a federal law here. You're not talking about NCAA law. And if we get to this point, I mean, you know, it has to be upheld. I mean, this is a pretty sticky situation when, you know, a law is a law. And again, it has to go through a lot of parameters. It's got to be voted on, discussed, debated on the floor house Senate, then has to be signed with the President of the United States. And so, There's a lot of things that you have to do to make this work. That's the reason I said earlier. One person can't do it. I could have gone after and tried to do this on my own. I may not have been wasting my time because you have to have help from the other party. And Joe Manchin is obviously a guy that's got a lot of things done",
